\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Per-layer timing (ms, log scale) for trotter (solid) and BP (dashed) on MI100, $4\times 4$ grid, for $\chi \in \{50, 80, 120\}$. Three regimes are visible: a ramp-up phase (layers 1--12) where the live bond dimension grows from 1 to $\chi _{\mathrm  {max}}$ and cost rises steeply; a saturation plateau where trotter stabilizes at $\sim $13{,}000, $\sim $55{,}000, and $\sim $243{,}000\,ms respectively; and BP spikes at hard layers where adaptive convergence requires the full 100-sweep budget. At $\chi =120$, BP exceeds trotter by up to $5\times $ at layers 16 and 20, making BP the dominant cost at large bond dimension.}}{22}{figure.caption.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:breakdown}{{3}{22}{Per-layer timing (ms, log scale) for trotter (solid) and BP (dashed) on MI100, $4\times 4$ grid, for $\chi \in \{50, 80, 120\}$. Three regimes are visible: a ramp-up phase (layers 1--12) where the live bond dimension grows from 1 to $\chimax $ and cost rises steeply; a saturation plateau where trotter stabilizes at $\sim $13{,}000, $\sim $55{,}000, and $\sim $243{,}000\,ms respectively; and BP spikes at hard layers where adaptive convergence requires the full 100-sweep budget. At $\chi =120$, BP exceeds trotter by up to $5\times $ at layers 16 and 20, making BP the dominant cost at large bond dimension}{figure.caption.21}{}}
